The previous fix (sizing the header to the URL's own aspect ratio)
only knew the file's shape - it couldn't see a square avatar that's
actually a non-square photo pillarboxed onto a square canvas, black
bars baked directly into the pixels. No CDN resize parameter can
reveal or remove that, since it isn't a shape the file has, only the
picture within it does.

Bitmap.detectLetterboxContentBounds() (YouTubeUtils.kt) decodes the
thumbnail once and samples inward from each edge for solid near-black
bars (not just a dark scene - nearly every sampled pixel across a row/
column has to clear the threshold), capped at 40% trim per edge so a
photo that's simply dark near a border isn't mistaken for one that's
mostly letterboxing. When it finds real bars, the artist header now
crops to that content region and sizes itself to the cropped image's
own aspect ratio; when it doesn't (most images), the existing
URL-aspect-ratio path is unchanged.
