From 1fdee942e9a50f4ac6873f58d20cbae383b8a30a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 13:11:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 115/249] Proactively refresh the streaming PoToken before it
 actually goes stale

Confirmed the CDN-connection-duration theory wrong: tightening chunk size
and adding an OkHttp callTimeout, independently, both left the failure at
exactly the same ~60-64s wall-clock point with the same 403. Since neither
touched connection duration and the timing didn't move, duration was never
the actual variable - time since the credentials were minted is.

songUrlCache trusted a cached URL for streamExpiresInSeconds (YouTube's own
claim, routinely hours), but the streaming PoToken embedded in that URL
has a real-world validity far shorter. Worse, PoTokenGenerator caches the
streaming pot at the session level, so simply calling
playerResponseForPlayback again on refresh had no guarantee of minting a
new one - it could easily hand back the same one that's about to expire.

Caps the cache's effective trust at 40s regardless of what
streamExpiresInSeconds claims, and forces invalidatePoTokenSession() before
any refresh (skipped on a song's genuinely first resolve, so that one
doesn't pay for a rotation it doesn't need). CHUNK_LENGTH tightened to
256KB so no single request - even for a low-bitrate song - is likely to
still be mid-transfer when the old credentials actually go bad.

