From 11a913398b5d4ee3f56e1f17cefa9bd4dbecbbcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 21:49:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 121/249] Replay real playback-start telemetry: cpn +
 watchtime/atr pings

OuterTune's only playback ping fired at a song's END for local history
purposes, and the resolved stream URL never carried a cpn at all -
decoupled from the telemetry a real YouTube Music client sends the
moment a song actually starts (an initial "playback" hit, a watchtime
checkpoint at ~5.54s, an "atr" ping 5s later, then a second checkpoint),
all tied together by one cpn shared with the audio request itself.
SimpMusic (a sibling YTM client) replays this exact sequence; Metrolist
doesn't, so this is unconfirmed as a bot-check fix, but it's a real gap
between our traffic and a real client's.

YTPlayerUtils now mints one cpn per resolve, embeds it in the stream URL
(matching how SimpMusic does it), and carries it plus the playbackTracking
URLs through PlaybackData/CachedStreamUrl. MusicService fires the sequence
exactly once per song - whether the stream came from a fresh resolve, a
precached entry, or a cache hit - via a new maybeSendPlaybackTelemetry(),
gated by telemetrySentForMediaId so a mid-song PoToken refresh doesn't
refire it. The existing end-of-song history-registration ping is
untouched; this is additive.

