From fa2579b2c25a0a7337cadfae1d4920a56848697f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 20:25:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 153/249] Fix a crash-loop on launch: recreate the database on
 a downgrade instead of crashing

Logcat: "IllegalStateException: A migration from 24 to 23 was required but
not found" - FATAL, on every single launch. The device had a database left
behind at version 24 from an earlier local build; this patch line's current
version is 23, and Room has no migration path for going backwards, so it
crashed instead of starting.

This app is rebuilt from diverging local patch lines rather than one
monotonically increasing version history, so "the on-disk database is newer
than this build's schema" is a real, recurring situation here - not a
one-off. Added fallbackToDestructiveMigrationOnDowngrade so a downgrade
recreates the database instead of crash-looping the app into being
unusable; a migration path still wouldn't exist either way, so the choice
is between a working app with a reset library and a completely unusable
one. Left off newTestInstance, which exists specifically to check a
restored backup's real compatibility during Restore - this would silently
paper over exactly what that check needs to catch.
---
 .../main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/db/MusicDatabase.kt | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/db/MusicDatabase.kt b/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/db/MusicDatabase.kt
index 2217a4d2..93349a54 100644
--- a/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/db/MusicDatabase.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/db/MusicDatabase.kt
@@ -138,6 +138,16 @@ abstract class InternalDatabase : RoomDatabase() {
                     .addMigrations(MIGRATION_16_17)
                     .addMigrations(MIGRATION_21_22)
                     .addMigrations(MIGRATION_22_23)
+                    // This app gets rebuilt from different, sometimes-diverging local patch
+                    // lines rather than shipping one monotonically-increasing version history,
+                    // so the database on disk can genuinely be a newer version than the build
+                    // now running knows about (e.g. after switching back to an older patch
+                    // line). With no migration path for that direction, Room's only other
+                    // option is to crash on every single launch - recreating the database
+                    // instead at least leaves the app usable. Not applied to newTestInstance:
+                    // that one exists specifically to check a restored backup's real
+                    // compatibility, which this would silently paper over.
+                    .fallbackToDestructiveMigrationOnDowngrade(dropAllTables = true)
                     .build()
             )
 
