Subject: [PATCH 177/249] Restore the Core-v0.18.2 stream architecture, and
 drop the throttle cooldown

Two things, both undoing my own damage from earlier in this session.

First, the actual regression. The original VISIONOS port (Core-v0.18.2)
included making PlayerResponse.VideoDetails' optional fields nullable,
because VISIONOS answers with a trimmed videoDetails and requiring those
fields makes the whole response fail to deserialize - silently dropping the
client. That commit was among the five lost from this branch's history; when
recovering them I judged "a VISIONOS client change" unrelated to the version
bump I was chasing and skipped it. Commits 169-170 later re-added VISIONOS
without the nullability change, so VISIONOS has been discarded before use
ever since, and every "fix" after that was chasing a symptom of the omission.

The chain and ordering are now exactly as they were in v0.18.2: VISIONOS
first (-2), MAIN_CLIENT/WEB_REMIX second (-1), then ANDROID_VR_NO_AUTH,
ANDROID_VR 1.65.10, ANDROID_VR 1.43.32, IOS. The interim reshuffles -
pulling WEB_REMIX out of the chain, then re-adding it last, trimming the
fallbacks - were all attempts to route around a failure whose cause was the
missing nullability, and none of them belong.

Second, the cooldown. Detecting Google's network-level "automated queries"
throttle and then refusing to make player requests for five minutes turned a
throttle that may already have lapsed into guaranteed silence, and it was
never a thing in the original client. The detection stays, as a log line
only: naming that response is genuinely useful, since it looks nothing like a
per-video rejection. Acting on it was the mistake.

Kept from the intervening work only what stands on its own: the single
first-byte validation probe (the second probe I added was blowing the
player's own timeout), the retry cap at 2 rather than 5, and per-client
logging at warn so a release logcat shows which client was tried and why it
was dropped.
