From d1c06766769361dd474e3de2086956ea6f63c57b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:03:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 200/249] Stop the EQ distorting everything, and run the bands
 it was skipping

Five faults, found reading the signal path from queueInput outwards.

tanh on every sample. The soft clip was applied unconditionally, and tanh
is not the identity anywhere: tanh(0.5) is 0.462, tanh(0.7) is 0.604. So
ordinary material at a normal level was pulled down 8-14% - continuous,
waveform-dependent odd-harmonic distortion across the whole signal rather
than something that engages on peaks. Worst on bass, whose high-amplitude
fundamental throws harmonics up into the midrange, and worst of all with a
boosted band, since the pre-gain leaves that band's output riding right
where tanh starts bending hard. That is the crackle blamed on the bands.
It now passes through untouched below 0.75 and only saturates above it,
the two pieces meeting with matching value and slope.

Denormals in the delay lines. An IIR's feedback rings down through ever
smaller values on a fade-out and then sits in denormals, which a lot of
ARM hardware handles off the fast path at orders of magnitude the cost.
Sixteen bands per channel per sample is then enough to miss the audio
deadline - and to miss it per band, since the bands that ring longest are
the low, high-Q ones. Flushed below -500dBFS, in the biquad and in the
compressor's envelope.

A log10 and a pow per sample in the compressor, both computed even when
below the threshold and doing nothing. Rewritten in the linear domain
where the whole dB-domain knee is one power - (Tlin/env)^(1-1/ratio) -
and only reached while actually compressing.

Truncation to 16-bit. toInt() rounds towards zero, so the error is
consistently signed and behaves as waveform-correlated distortion rather
than noise. Rounds now, and clamps: a value reaching +32768 wrapped to
full-scale negative, which is the loudest click a sample can make.

Coefficients substituted between two samples. Not resetting filter state
on a gain change avoided one click but not this one - a whole new
coefficient set mid-stream is itself a step in the response, one per
slider update. Interpolated over 20ms instead, and snapped on flush.

Two features that did not work at all:

Low-pass and high-pass bands were never audible. They are offered in the
panel and produced by AutoEQ's LP/HP filters, and forBand builds them
correctly - but they have no gain, so gainDb sits at 0, and the bypass
gate's `gainDb == 0f` test read them as flat and bulk-copied the buffer.
A curve made only of pass filters did nothing whatsoever.

AutoEQ's Preamp was added to every band's gain. It is one global gain
stage, not a per-band offset: a peaking filter's gain only acts near its
own centre, so shifting all of them turned every band AutoEQ specified at
0dB into a real cut and left a comb of dips instead of a level change -
while LP/HP bands, having no gain, kept their original level. Dropped;
the processor's own pre-gain already derives that headroom globally.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
