From e4603d4cfa95d31cf8231505f7834d782d87b217 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:23:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 201/249] Stop rotating visitorData while signed in - it was
 flagging the session

The logcat from the previous build shows every fallback client answering
LOGIN_REQUIRED / 'Sign in to confirm you are not a bot', and WEB_REMIX
resolving a url with its gvs pot present that is then refused with a 403
on the first media request. That is not a token that is missing or bound
wrongly. That is the session identity being rejected.

rotateSessionIdentity minted a fresh visitorData and stored it. When
signed out that is harmless - there is no other half to contradict. When
signed in it is not: the visitorData issued during login belongs with the
account cookie, and YouTube.visitorData() returns a brand new anonymous
one scraped from getSwJsData. Overwriting the first with the second
leaves the cookie asserting one identity and the visitorData another,
which is exactly what bot detection is looking for.

It was called from onPlayerError, so it ran on every source error,
including the ones it had itself caused. The first rotation poisoned the
session, every track then failed, and every failure rotated again. That
loop has no recovery path, which is why this presented as the app dying
rather than as intermittent failures - and why it stayed dead across
restarts, since the poisoned visitorData is persisted.

Metrolist, on this same account and device, never rotates: App.kt takes
the stored visitorData and only mints one when there is none. Its login
screen captures visitorData from the login page itself, alongside the
cookie, so the pair always agrees.

Signed in, this now invalidates the PoToken generator and stops there -
still discarding every cached token and rebuilding the WebView, which is
what clears a stale or rejected token, without touching the identity the
account is tied to. Signed out, the old behaviour is kept.

An already-poisoned visitorData is still stored, so an affected install
needs one sign out and back in to get a login-issued one again.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../dd3boh/outertune/utils/YTPlayerUtils.kt   | 32 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/utils/YTPlayerUtils.kt b/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/utils/YTPlayerUtils.kt
index cd2cd67e..db5e7793 100644
--- a/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/utils/YTPlayerUtils.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/java/com/dd3boh/outertune/utils/YTPlayerUtils.kt
@@ -447,14 +447,36 @@ object YTPlayerUtils {
      * targets a third, distinct cause - YouTube's bot-detection having flagged this client's
      * identity, which a differently-resolved URL under the *same* identity would not fix.
      *
-     * Not gated on login state: every PoToken here is bound to visitorData or to a video id, and
-     * never to the account's dataSyncId, so a fresh visitorData genuinely does buy a new BotGuard
-     * identity whether or not the session is signed in. Invalidating the generator alongside it
-     * discards every cached token and rebuilds the WebView, which is what clears a merely stale
-     * token as opposed to a flagged identity.
+     * Gated on login state, which it did not used to be. The note that replaced said a fresh
+     * visitorData buys a new BotGuard identity "whether or not the session is signed in". It does
+     * not: when signed in, visitorData is not a free-floating anonymous id but half of a pair. The
+     * one issued during login belongs with the account cookie, and [YouTube.visitorData] mints a
+     * brand new *anonymous* one from getSwJsData. Overwriting the login-issued value therefore
+     * leaves the cookie claiming one identity and the visitorData another, which is precisely the
+     * shape YouTube's bot detection looks for - every client then comes back LOGIN_REQUIRED /
+     * "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot", and the web stream url is signed but answered with a
