From 98c60f82e7e8580697ffcb776b3022395d2cefd6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:25:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 203/249] Pass innertubex the five arguments it takes, not two

extract() takes videoId, hints, excludedClients, audioQuality and
clientPlaybackNonce. This passed the first two and left the rest on their
defaults, and compared against Metrolist - which drives the same library
successfully on the same account - every one of those defaults was wrong
here.

allowSabr and allowHls both default to true in ContentHints. This app has
no SABR support at all; MetrolistStreamResolver's own class doc says so.
A SABR url is a bootstrap for a protocol the player cannot speak, so
handing one to a plain OkHttp data source does not error, it stalls - the
track buffers to wherever the first fetch reached and then waits there
forever. That is the stop at about a minute, which never produced a
PlaybackException because nothing failed: logcat shows BUFFERING at
position 63s repeating every three seconds with no error at all. HLS is
the same story, since nothing here reads a media playlist either. Both
are now off, with a sabrBootstrap check as a second line of defence.

allowBoundedRange also defaults to true, and a download has to turn it
off. A client whose url is only served for an explicitly bounded range
cannot hand over a whole file in one request, and one whole-file request
is the only kind DownloadUtil makes - so whenever selection landed on
such a client the download was refused a few seconds in, and whenever it
happened to land elsewhere the download succeeded. That is exactly the
observed pattern of near-total failure with the occasional song getting
through. Metrolist passes allowBoundedRange = false for downloads and
true for playback, which is what this does now.

audioQuality was never passed, so the user's quality preference reached
the app's own client loop and was ignored by this engine entirely.

clientPlaybackNonce was never passed either, so the extractor minted its
own and the cpn on the stream url disagreed with the cpn the telemetry
pings are fired under - the two have to match for a play to register.

excludedClients replaces the post-hoc rejection added in the previous
commit. Refusing WEB_REMIX after the fact threw away a whole extraction
and could leave nothing to play; excluding it up front is what the
parameter is for and leaves innertubex free to answer with another
client.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
