From a00aa23ea6c0cf02072ab4553744202d4f62508f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 15:31:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 205/249] Take the stream engine from Metrolist v13.6.3
 wholesale

Every stream fix in this backlog has been tuning innertubex - token
bindings, allowSabr, excludedClients, the cipher registry url - and each
one moved the symptom without curing it. Playback kept dying at 1:03.

A logcat taken from Metrolist on this same device and account, playing at
the same minute, explains why. It resolved and played past a minute with
zero 403s, and its log opens with this:

  Metrolist_CipherDeobfusc: Initializing PlayerConfigStore (bundled + cached overlay)
  Metrolist_CipherConfig:   Loaded bundled configs (440 hashes)
  Metrolist_CipherConfig:   Overlaying cached Faraday configs (644 hashes)
  Metrolist_CipherConfig:   Overlaying cached Zemer configs (548 hashes)
  Metrolist_CipherDeobfusc: Prewarming cipher WebView

None of that exists in innertubex. The installed, working Metrolist is
v13.6.3, and it contains no reference to innertubex at all: it does its
own player.js deobfuscation in a WebView, over a config table with a
bundled asset as its floor and both registries overlaid on top. So this
app has been resolving streams with a fundamentally different and weaker
mechanism than the app it was being compared against, and no amount of
parameter tuning was going to close that gap.

Worth recording that current Metrolist master has since removed
CipherDeobfuscator in favour of innertubex - the design that is failing
here. Porting from master would have reproduced the bug. The source taken
is the v13.6.3 tag, which is what the working apk on the device is built
from.

Imported, with package and log-tag rewrites only:

  cipher/  CipherDeobfuscator, CipherWebView, FunctionNameExtractor,
           PlayerConfigParser, PlayerConfigStore, PlayerDatesStore,
           PlayerJsFetcher, RendererRecoveryPolicy
  potoken/ PoTokenWebView, PoTokenGenerator, JavaScriptUtil,
           PoTokenException, PoTokenResult
  YTPlayerUtils, and assets/solver (meriyah + astring + yt.solver.core)

Kept deliberately alongside it, because the imported engine has no
equivalent and these are this app's own: PlaybackData.cpn, so the nonce
on the stream url still matches the one telemetry pings are fired under;
invalidatePoTokenSession; and rotateSessionIdentity including the guard
that stops it overwriting a login-issued visitorData while signed in.

Removed: MetrolistStreamResolver, StreamRangePolicy, PlayerCipherConfig,
SignatureCipherManager, and the innertubex dependency itself - nothing
links against it now, so it was only costing apk size.

MusicService's per-client range policy goes with it. v13.6.3 does not
have one: a fresh resolve is bounded flat at CHUNK_LENGTH from
dataSpec.uriPositionOffset, a cached url and a download are read straight
through, and headers are merged onto the caller's rather than replacing
them. That is now what this does too.

Small additions to :innertube to support the import: Format.cipher,
Format.audioTrack and Format.isOriginal so auto-dubbed tracks can be
filtered; YouTubeClient.requirePoToken; YouTube.newPipePlayer and
getNewPipeStreamUrls for the NewPipe graft path; and the
ContentAwareFallbackStrategy it picks clients with.

R8 keep rules added for both WebView bridges. Their members are reached
only by name from page JavaScript, so R8 sees no caller and strips them,
and the failure is silent - the WebView loads and the callback never
fires.

Verified by assembling coreRelease through R8 (packaging then fails only
on the absent local keystore, which CI supplies).

