From 5056fb9e4ae4ff77db0318908e53f30d01bd9c29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:34:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 216/249] Keep the credit whose channel actually has the song,
 not just the real one

Two credits reading the same name are one artist listed twice, whatever
ids they carry. That happens after a rebrand once the real channel's name
catches up with the placeholder's - both then read i-dle - and it happens
when a song is credited to both an artist's real channel and the
auto-generated Topic channel YouTube made for them, which share a name
and differ only by id.

The previous rule only handled the first of those, because it could only
drop a local placeholder. Two real channels with one name went straight
through, which is the shape behind a credit that reads correctly and
opens a page that is not the artist's.

Which to keep is not arbitrary. It is the one whose channel actually
carries this song, because that is the page someone tapping the name is
trying to reach, and that is what resolving the name establishes: search
answers with the channel YouTube itself considers canonical for it, which
is the one holding the catalogue. A credit matching that wins. Failing
that a real channel beats a local placeholder, which has no page at all
and so cannot be holding anything.

Costs one search per duplicated name, and only for a song that actually
has a duplicate.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

