From 1f120dc8d1674ad2d1c71ffe28acd1fbc2de33e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:41:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 217/249] Give the artist header a fixed square and crop into
 it

Following Metrolist's placement, which is why its artist pages look
settled where this one did not.

What was here tried to honour whatever shape each source image happened
to be. It read the natural aspect ratio off the url, decoded the bitmap
to hunt for letterbox bars baked into the pixels, cropped those away,
then scaled the result by one of three boost factors depending on what it
had found, and clamped the outcome between 0.6 and 1.6.

Every step of that is defensible on its own and the sum of them was not,
because the header ended up a different height on every artist. Nothing
else on the screen could be positioned against it, the name landed
somewhere different each time, and the page visibly reflowed when the
bitmap decode finished and the ratio changed underneath it.

A constant square, filled by ContentScale.Crop, gives up showing every
image whole. That is a real cost and a small one - these are portraits
and channel banners, and the middle of one is the part worth seeing -
and in exchange the header is the same size for every artist, the name
always lands in the same place, and nothing moves after load.

Cropped from the top rather than the centre, so a standing photo keeps
the head instead of the chin.

Removes the letterbox detection, the natural-aspect read, the three boost
constants and the bitmap decode that fed them.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

