From 6c818ea9392b8df2e2f957f215a9378b8d1371f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:14:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 219/249] Let Stats count plays from this account's other
 devices

Stats could only see what this install recorded, so listening done in the
YouTube Music app or on another phone counted for nothing. A switch under
Settings > Player > History now folds YouTube's own history in.

Time comes from the song's length, not from the history, because the
history carries no duration - only coarse "Today" / "This week" section
labels. That is sound rather than a fudge: YouTube writes a history entry
only once a song has finished, so an entry is a completed play and the
time it represents is the track's full length. A four minute song
appearing five times is twenty minutes.

Songs never played on this device have no local row, so they are built
from the history entry's own metadata - which carries title, artists with
their ids, album and duration - and held in memory for the ranking only.
Nothing is written to the library: turning up in someone's statistics is
not a reason to start claiming they own a song. Songs that do have a
local row keep it and gain the remote plays on top.

The local query asks for a wider pool than the six rows displayed before
merging. Adding remote plays to an already-trimmed top six would let a
song sitting seventh locally, but first once everything is counted, stay
invisible.

Off by default, and off it costs a lookup against an empty map. On, it
pages history sequentially, capped at 40 pages - history has no end, and
paging to it would be an unbounded number of round trips for a
diminishing tail.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
