From ceae56e93952ea1b40c41e100c0f549107eef7b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 14:24:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 226/249] Keep a song's two records of its album from
 disagreeing

View album on a song opened a different album from the one the player
opens for the same song, and from the album screen the song looked fine.

A song's album is recorded twice: in song_album_map, and denormalised onto
the song row as albumId. Merging two albums - which the local scanner does
when it finds duplicates - rewrote only the map. Nothing has ever rewritten
the column, so it kept pointing at an album that gets deleted two lines
later.

Which album a song belonged to then depended on which copy a screen
happened to read. MediaMetadata prefers the relation, so the player opened
the right one. The song menu read the column, so it opened the album that
no longer exists - and the navigation still worked, because a merged album
id usually still resolves to something.

Both halves fixed. The merge now moves the column alongside the map, so
the two stop drifting apart in the first place. And the song menu resolves
its album the same way MediaMetadata does, relation first and column
second, so the menu and the player cannot disagree even about rows that
already drifted. The menu's visibility check reads the same way, since
gating on the column alone would hide the item for a song whose album is
only recorded in the map.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
