From a40bbcdaabea6ae001c7e36a24da4de24e78ad1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:54:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 227/249] Merge the prefixed artist rows, not just the credits
 pointing at them

Rewriting credits was never going to hold, and this is why. The artist
rows stored under the MPLA spelling survived in the artist table, and the
song sync looks an artist up by name before it considers the id it was
handed:

    artistByNameIgnoreCase(cleanName)?.id ?: artist.id ?: generate()

So a surviving prefixed row named i-dle was found by that lookup and its
id handed straight back, putting the prefixed credit onto the song again.
Normalising the incoming id never got a look in, because the incoming id
was not what was being used. Every sync undid the previous repair, which
is exactly the reverting and the returning duplicates.

The rows themselves are now merged into the bare-id row for the same
channel and deleted, so that lookup has nothing prefixed left to return.
Colliding map rows are removed before the rest are moved: song_artist_map
is keyed on (songId, artistId), so a song credited under both spellings
cannot simply have one repointed onto the other.

The lookup result is normalised too, rather than only the incoming id -
cheap, and it means a prefixed row reappearing from anywhere still cannot
reach a credit. The artists sync builds its rows from the remote id
directly, so that is normalised at the point of construction as well.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

