From da7ab617c551fa6a1c7016476297229f547a8b2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:58:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 228/249] Model the actual instability, cap the frame rate, and
 make removals undoable

Four unrelated pieces of work.

The ferrofluid renderer was a ring of spheres orbiting each other, which is
the generic Shadertoy metaball look rather than anything ferrofluid does.
Real ferrofluid in a vertical magnetic field undergoes the Rosensweig
instability: past a critical field strength the flat surface breaks into a
regular hexagonal lattice of peaks, that being the packing that best
balances surface tension, gravity and the field. So the scene is now a
two-ring hexagonal lattice (1 + 6 + 12) of cusped peaks rising out of a
shallow pool, with bass standing in for field strength - quiet leaves the
surface near flat, loud drives the peaks up. The peaks are an exact rounded
cone SDF rather than spheres, because a real peak has a broad foot and a
near-point tip and blending spheres can only ever give mounds; the smin
radius is tight for the same reason.

The material was rebuilt too. Ferrofluid is magnetite in oil, so optically
it is opaque black and essentially everything visible in real footage is
reflection rather than diffuse shading: two sharp highlights, a Schlick
Fresnel edge, and a reflected vertical gradient standing in for the room.
That last one is two lines and does more for "liquid mirror" than any
amount of extra lighting, because what makes a mirror look like a mirror is
having something to reflect. The body is then deliberately tinted with the
theme colour against physical accuracy - a black shape on a dark UI is just
a hole, and the specular model is what identifies the material anyway.

The render loop advanced time on every vsync, so the full per-pixel march
ran at whatever the display does; a 120Hz phone paid twice the GPU of a
60Hz one to animate a slow, heavily blurred blob that reads no differently
either way. Frames are now dropped to a per-tier cap without touching the
state, so no redraw happens for them. Time still comes from the vsync stamp
rather than accumulating kept frames, so this slows the render rate without
slowing the animation. A ULTRA tier is added for GPUs that want the cost.

Downloads: per-file speed was already handled by the &range trick and
stream resolution is cached, so the only lever left on a queue is how many
run at once, 3 -> 5. Deliberately not higher - each concurrent download is
another googlevideo connection carrying the same PoToken.

Swiping a song out of the queue was silent and irreversible. It now says so
and offers Undo, capturing the index and the queue up front so the song
goes back where it was, and so a queue switch in the meantime cannot drop
it into an unrelated list.

Verified by compiling coreRelease. The AGSL itself compiles on device
rather than at build time; it falls back to the Canvas version on failure.

