From e3a4f90ba3de659d4c1e77c6fea30410f72d4fd8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:02:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 233/249] Move songs between the play cache and downloads
 instead of duplicating them

There are two caches: the play cache, which is LRU-evicted and holds
whatever has been streamed, and the download cache, which is not evicted.
Playback reads the download cache first and falls through to the play cache
on a miss.

That means downloading a song you had just listened to fetched the whole
thing again over the network, and then kept both copies - the download
cache's, and the play cache's, which lingers until the evictor gets round
to it. The bytes are identical and were already on the device.

Downloading now seeds the download cache from the play cache first, via
CacheWriter with the play cache as a terminal upstream. This runs before
the download request rather than instead of it: Media3 still owns the
download, since its index is what the UI's downloaded state is read from,
so the request is still sent and simply finds everything cached and
completes without touching the network. Seeding rather than
short-circuiting keeps one download path instead of two, and a partial or
failed transfer degrades into an ordinary download.

Coverage is checked rather than assumed - a song skipped halfway through
leaves a real but incomplete cache entry, and treating that as a copy would
produce a truncated download. The play cache is also given no upstream of
its own, so a wrong coverage check fails the read instead of quietly
writing a hole.

The reverse direction is handled on completion: any download, promoted or
fetched, releases its play cache copy. Once a song is downloaded that copy
can never be read again, so it is only taking LRU budget from songs that
are not downloaded. Best-effort, since removeResource throws on a locked
span - precisely the case where the player is reading it right now - and
failing there just leaves the copy for the evictor.

Also fixes a latent bug in getFromCache, which concatenated spans straight
out of getCachedSpans. That returns a Set, so iteration order is not file
order, and a song held as more than one span (anything paused, seeked or
resumed while caching) was reassembled with its pieces shuffled: a file of
exactly the right size that does not play. Sorted by position now.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

