From de775b79db287a61fd671b8b874341a741fcc69f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:59:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 241/249] Make the ferrofluid actually follow the music, and
 let that be tuned

The audio was barely driving anything. Spread - the number that decides
whether droplets are inside each other's blend range, and so the entire
break-up-and-remerge behaviour - was a slow sine swinging over 0.40 with
bass allowed a 45% nudge on top. The shape was really just breathing on a
timer with a faint wobble, which is why it read as ambient rather than
reactive.

The timer is now the small term and the track is the large one: a gentle
idle drift so a paused or quiet song still moves, plus a bass/transient
drive worth more than twice it. The mass visibly flies apart on a loud
passage and gathers back in a quiet one. Transients also swell the droplets
and the core, so a beat reads as a pulse as well as a push outward, and
treble drives the rim colour several times harder than before.

Reactivity is a setting because it is a taste question, not a cost one - it
changes how far droplets travel for a given amount of music, not how
expensive a frame is to draw. Subtle is roughly where this sat before, for
a background that stays in the background; Extreme is for watching it.

The drive term is clamped, so turning reactivity up raises how hard normal
material hits rather than letting a loud peak throw droplets outside the
bounding sphere and clip them. The sphere is widened to 1.40 to cover the
new maximum reach of ~1.16 with margin.

Verified by compiling coreRelease. The AGSL compiles on device, not at
build time; it falls back to the Canvas version on failure.

