From 186cdc9875a91b7a6c96202d88adcf837692a23b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:07:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 248/249] Prove pure-Java playback is possible, so the
 packaging choice is a real one

The previous probe found that the obstacle to desktop playback was the
container rather than the codec: everything YouTube serves is fragmented
MP4, whose samples live in moof/trun boxes rather than the moov sample
table an ordinary reader walks, so the metadata parsed and there was
nothing to decode. That left the packaging question open - one
self-contained jar, or a native library bundled per architecture - and it
is not a question worth deciding on a guess.

This settles it by doing the whole thing: fetch, demux, decode, count PCM.
mp4parser reads the fragments, the decoder is configured from the sample
entry's own esds rather than from an assumption, the trun sample sizes are
walked against the mdat payloads to cut the blob into frames, and jaad
turns those frames into PCM.

On Windows 11: 22 fragments, 9177 samples, 400 frames decoded to 1638400
bytes of PCM at 44100Hz stereo. That figure is worth checking rather than
just reporting - 400 frames x 1024 samples x 2 channels x 2 bytes is
exactly 1638400, so the decoder produced correctly sized audio rather than
plausible-looking noise.

So both routes are open, and choosing between a single jar and a native
library is now a matter of preference rather than possibility.

